{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01G3gsb6a226mCpGnXL6osxN","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tslug: pose-shared-runtime\n3\tstatus: draft\n4\tcreated_at: 2026-10-13\n5\tdepends_on:\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t# Roadmap: Domínio e runtime comuns com desempenho qualificado\n9\t\n10\t**Resultado:** operações CLI/MCP coerentes e custo controlado por produtor,\n11\tcom reuso limitado e runtime opcional somente após qualificação de identidade,\n12\tcoerência, autorização e recuperação. Consultar a [matriz](../results/pose-implementation-plan.json)\n13\te a [análise de performance](../reports/2026-10-12-pose-front-08-resident-performance.md).\n14\t\n15\t**Estado (2026-11-10):** os quatro milestones estão fechados com revisão independente\n16\t(B32 operation-performance, B31 common-domain-handoff, B33 bounded-graph-caches e B35\n17\toptional-worktree-runtime, esta em três fatias medidas), com o runtime opt-in e standalone\n18\tpreservado. O que o roadmap não entrega (cota e afinidade de CPU, limites por comando,\n19\tnamed pipe no Windows, roteamento dos checks para o runtime por padrão) está em specs\n20\trascunho próprias, fora deste roadmap. A revisão do roadmap é feita sobre o estado congelado deste commit (sem mudar a matriz de validação nem a política até o fechamento). A chave do memo residente passou a conter a configuração efetiva do Git (revisão do roadmap, achado F1).\n21\t\n22\t## Milestone: operation-performance\n23\t- after: spec:pose-check-spawns-fewer-git-processes, spec:pose-attention-within-a-second\n24\t- target_start:\n25\t- target_due:\n26\t- specs: pose-governance-operation-performance\n27\t\n28\t**Exit gate:** B32 mede por produtor e reusa catálogo/bundles/Git por operação,\n29\tcom clustering fiel e benchmark isolado que retém verdict/ambiente e não desfaz\n30\tarquivos do operador. Consultar o [lote 7](../reports/2026-10-13-pose-batch-7-authoring.md).\n31\tB32 é raiz independente de B14/B15; desempenho não autoriza gates sobre dados stale.\n32\t\n33\t\n34\t## Continuação prospectiva — lote 14\n35\t\n36\tConsultar o [lote 14](../reports/2026-10-14-pose-batch-14-authoring.md) e o\n37\t[ADR de domínio/runtime Proposed](../adr/2026-10-14-shared-domain-and-optional-worktree-runtime.md).\n38\tResolver interface/IPC/ownership/compatibilidade e budgets antes de start.\n39\tB31/B33 não exigem B35; runtime consome ambos após seus demais pré-requisitos.\n40\t\n41\t## Milestone: common-domain-handoff\n42\t- after: spec:pose-cli-mcp-operation-results, spec:pose-writer-coordination-and-atomic-publication, spec:pose-governance-read-view-generation, spec:pose-recoverable-closeout-plan, spec:pose-mcp-active-context, spec:pose-safe-validate-orchestration\n43\t- target_start:\n44\t- target_due:\n45\t- specs: pose-common-domain-and-cli-mcp-handoff\n46\t\n47\t**Exit gate:** B31 preserva resultados/erros/authority por fonte de domínio comum;\n48\twrappers extraídos não spawnam POSE. P04 tem route/context compatível ou limitação\n49\texplícita; MCP oferece plan read-only/handoff tipado, conservando P15. Writes\n50\tmantêm prova/coordenação e Harness não é substituído por execução arbitrária.\n51\t\n52\t## Milestone: bounded-graph-caches\n53\t- after: spec:pose-index-generation-integrity, spec:pose-governance-read-view-generation, spec:pose-delivery-integrity-index-compaction\n54\t- target_start:\n55\t- target_due:\n56\t- specs: pose-bounded-isolated-graph-caches\n57\t\n58\t**Exit gate:** B33 conserva grafo/filtragem com isolamento profundo, mutation/race\n59\te redução medida de alocações/RAM; bytes/pins/waiters têm orçamento por worktree\n60\te global. Misses coordenadas produzem uma vez por chave; nenhuma contaminação,\n61\texpansão total desnecessária ou cache de autorização stale. Git reader fica scoped.\n62\t\n63\t## Milestone: optional-worktree-runtime\n64\t- after: operation-performance, common-domain-handoff, bounded-graph-caches, spec:pose-git-and-dispatch-snapshot-integrity, spec:pose-cli-mcp-operation-results, spec:pose-writer-coordination-and-atomic-publication, spec:pose-index-generation-integrity, spec:pose-governance-read-view-generation, spec:pose-attention-within-a-second\n65\t- target_start:\n66\t- target_due:\n67\t- specs: pose-optional-worktree-runtime, pose-worktree-runtime-resident-cache, pose-worktree-runtime-qos-and-measurement\n68\t\n69\t**Entrega (2026-11-10):** as três fatias estão fechadas (`pose-optional-worktree-runtime`,\n70\t`pose-worktree-runtime-resident-cache`, `pose-worktree-runtime-qos-and-measurement`), com as duas\n71\tspecs de engine que as medições pediram (`pose-index-input-binding-reads-each-transfer-once`,\n72\t`pose-governance-audit-skips-pure-git-reads`); o estado entregue e onde ele se afasta do ADR está na\n73\túltima emenda do ADR, e a medição em `.pose/reports/2026-11-09-pose-worktree-runtime-measurements.md`.\n74\tNão entregues: cota e afinidade de CPU e limites por comando (o runtime limita o que admite e guarda).\n75\tMedição: quatro execuções, a quarta qualifica (T1 a T7 passaram). O fechamento do milestone revisa o estado final dos três specs membros.\n76\t\n77\t**Exit gate:** B35 qualifica handshake user/worktree/Git/engine/schema, endpoint\n78\tprotegido, lifecycle/restart/update e fallback CI/standalone. Stdio não é IPC\n79\tcompartilhável; watcher é hint, writers publicam geração e efeitos ambíguos exigem\n80\treceipt antes de retry. CPU/jobs simultâneos preservam query/QoS/output determinístico\n81\tcom RAM/CPU/latência por cenário, sem scheduler/agents/workflows automáticos no core.\n82\t\n83\t## Ativação e encerramento\n84\t\n85\t- Manter draft durante a escrita; executar somente com escopo autorizado.\n86\t- Qualificar todos os deltas obrigatórios antes de encerrar o programa.\n87\t- Preservar verdict, coverage, bindings e limites de cada operação; latência menor não comprova eficácia universal.\n88\t- Conservar benchmarks isolados, resultados e erros; não restaurar trabalho do operador para medir.\n89\t- Adotar residência somente pelo contrato/qualificação correspondente, sem inferir que MCP stdio é IPC compartilhável.\n90\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"[private-session]","uuid":"2efda120-474b-4ad8-8289-c534894a08e7","timestamp":"2026-11-10T21:35:11.746Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/var/tmp/pose-work-go/pose-review-run-1183365784/worktree/.pose/roadmaps/pose-shared-runtime.md","content":"---\nslug: pose-shared-runtime\nstatus: draft\ncreated_at: 2026-10-13\ndepends_on:\n---\n\n# Roadmap: Domínio e runtime comuns com desempenho qualificado\n\n**Resultado:** operações CLI/MCP coerentes e custo controlado por produtor,\ncom reuso limitado e runtime opcional somente após qualificação de identidade,\ncoerência, autorização e recuperação. Consultar a [matriz](../results/pose-implementation-plan.json)\ne a [análise de performance](../reports/2026-10-12-pose-front-08-resident-performance.md).\n\n**Estado (2026-11-10):** os quatro milestones estão fechados com revisão independente\n(B32 operation-performance, B31 common-domain-handoff, B33 bounded-graph-caches e B35\noptional-worktree-runtime, esta em três fatias medidas), com o runtime opt-in e standalone\npreservado. O que o roadmap não entrega (cota e afinidade de CPU, limites por comando,\nnamed pipe no Windows, roteamento dos checks para o runtime por padrão) está em specs\nrascunho próprias, fora deste roadmap. A revisão do roadmap é feita sobre o estado congelado deste commit (sem mudar a matriz de validação nem a política até o fechamento). A chave do memo residente passou a conter a configuração efetiva do Git (revisão do roadmap, achado F1).\n\n## Milestone: operation-performance\n- after: spec:pose-check-spawns-fewer-git-processes, spec:pose-attention-within-a-second\n- target_start:\n- target_due:\n- specs: pose-governance-operation-performance\n\n**Exit gate:** B32 mede por produtor e reusa catálogo/bundles/Git por operação,\ncom clustering fiel e benchmark isolado que retém verdict/ambiente e não desfaz\narquivos do operador. Consultar o [lote 7](../reports/2026-10-13-pose-batch-7-authoring.md).\nB32 é raiz independente de B14/B15; desempenho não autoriza gates sobre dados stale.\n\n\n## Continuação prospectiva — lote 14\n\nConsultar o [lote 14](../reports/2026-10-14-pose-batch-14-authoring.md) e o\n[ADR de domínio/runtime Proposed](../adr/2026-10-14-shared-domain-and-optional-worktree-runtime.md).\nResolver interface/IPC/ownership/compatibilidade e budgets antes de start.\nB31/B33 não exigem B35; runtime consome ambos após seus demais pré-requisitos.\n\n## Milestone: common-domain-handoff\n- after: spec:pose-cli-mcp-operation-results, spec:pose-writer-coordination-and-atomic-publication, spec:pose-governance-read-view-generation, spec:pose-recoverable-closeout-plan, spec:pose-mcp-active-context, spec:pose-safe-validate-orchestration\n- target_start:\n- target_due:\n- specs: pose-common-domain-and-cli-mcp-handoff\n\n**Exit gate:** B31 preserva resultados/erros/authority por fonte de domínio comum;\nwrappers extraídos não spawnam POSE. P04 tem route/context compatível ou limitação\nexplícita; MCP oferece plan read-only/handoff tipado, conservando P15. Writes\nmantêm prova/coordenação e Harness não é substituído por execução arbitrária.\n\n## Milestone: bounded-graph-caches\n- after: spec:pose-index-generation-integrity, spec:pose-governance-read-view-generation, spec:pose-delivery-integrity-index-compaction\n- target_start:\n- target_due:\n- specs: pose-bounded-isolated-graph-caches\n\n**Exit gate:** B33 conserva grafo/filtragem com isolamento profundo, mutation/race\ne redução medida de alocações/RAM; bytes/pins/waiters têm orçamento por worktree\ne global. Misses coordenadas produzem uma vez por chave; nenhuma contaminação,\nexpansão total desnecessária ou cache de autorização stale. Git reader fica scoped.\n\n## Milestone: optional-worktree-runtime\n- after: operation-performance, common-domain-handoff, bounded-graph-caches, spec:pose-git-and-dispatch-snapshot-integrity, spec:pose-cli-mcp-operation-results, spec:pose-writer-coordination-and-atomic-publication, spec:pose-index-generation-integrity, spec:pose-governance-read-view-generation, spec:pose-attention-within-a-second\n- target_start:\n- target_due:\n- specs: pose-optional-worktree-runtime, pose-worktree-runtime-resident-cache, pose-worktree-runtime-qos-and-measurement\n\n**Entrega (2026-11-10):** as três fatias estão fechadas (`pose-optional-worktree-runtime`,\n`pose-worktree-runtime-resident-cache`, `pose-worktree-runtime-qos-and-measurement`), com as duas\nspecs de engine que as medições pediram (`pose-index-input-binding-reads-each-transfer-once`,\n`pose-governance-audit-skips-pure-git-reads`); o estado entregue e onde ele se afasta do ADR está na\núltima emenda do ADR, e a medição em `.pose/reports/2026-11-09-pose-worktree-runtime-measurements.md`.\nNão entregues: cota e afinidade de CPU e limites por comando (o runtime limita o que admite e guarda).\nMedição: quatro execuções, a quarta qualifica (T1 a T7 passaram). O fechamento do milestone revisa o estado final dos três specs membros.\n\n**Exit gate:** B35 qualifica handshake user/worktree/Git/engine/schema, endpoint\nprotegido, lifecycle/restart/update e fallback CI/standalone. Stdio não é IPC\ncompartilhável; watcher é hint, writers publicam geração e efeitos ambíguos exigem\nreceipt antes de retry. CPU/jobs simultâneos preservam query/QoS/output determinístico\ncom RAM/CPU/latência por cenário, sem scheduler/agents/workflows automáticos no core.\n\n## Ativação e encerramento\n\n- Manter draft durante a escrita; executar somente com escopo autorizado.\n- Qualificar todos os deltas obrigatórios antes de encerrar o programa.\n- Preservar verdict, coverage, bindings e limites de cada operação; latência menor não comprova eficácia universal.\n- Conservar benchmarks isolados, resultados e erros; não restaurar trabalho do operador para medir.\n- Adotar residência somente pelo contrato/qualificação correspondente, sem inferir que MCP stdio é IPC compartilhável.\n","numLines":90,"startLine":1,"totalLines":90}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01G3gsb6a226mCpGnXL6osxN","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cguy1Fsm6w2uiX2iVs1P4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014b2xYpw25BCDMfwatLf2U1","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/var/tmp/pose-work-go/pose-review-run-1183365784/worktree/.pose/adr/2026-10-14-shared-domain-and-optional-worktree-runtime.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4668,"cache_read_input_tokens":20588,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4668},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"[private-session]","uuid":"dc3770c6-4325-4bf8-b162-4149582c4693","timestamp":"2026-11-10T21:35:13.756Z","request_id":"req_011Cguy1FP1S7CGCcu5qCuZZ","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_014b2xYpw25BCDMfwatLf2U1":{"file_path":"/var/tmp/pose-work-go/pose-review-run-1183365784/worktree/.pose/adr/2026-10-14-shared-domain-and-optional-worktree-runtime.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014b2xYpw25BCDMfwatLf2U1","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# ADR: Shared domain and optional worktree runtime\n2\t\n3\t## Status\n4\t\n5\tAccepted for the delivered scope — 2026-11-10 (proposed 2026-10-14, for B31/B33/B35). The\n6\toptional worktree runtime (B35) was delivered in three slices, each reviewed independently, and\n7\tthe last amendment below states what stands and where it departs from the decisions above:\n8\ta protected Unix-socket endpoint with identity and handshake, five hosted read checks with typed\n9\tresults, a resident memo of pure Git reads, and admission lanes with a measurement. Not adopted,\n10\tand needing a spec of their own before they are claimed: CPU quota and affinity, per-command\n11\tmemory and I/O limits, a named pipe for Windows, routing the checks to the runtime by default.\n12\tPreserve accepted ADRs for active-context discovery, scoped Git readers, obligations and the\n13\tMCP-Conductor-Harness validation boundary.\n14\t\n15\t## Context\n16\t\n17\tThe [performance analysis](../reports/2026-10-12-pose-front-08-resident-performance.md)\n18\tseparates computation, transport and freshness costs. CLI-backed Store adapters\n19\tspawn the binary with a fixed deadline; MCP persistence alone does not remove\n20\trepeated algorithms or graph copies. The [UX analysis](../reports/2026-10-12-pose-front-07-operational-ux.md)\n21\tshows plan/context composition and planning handoff gaps while P15 read parity\n22\talready works. The [consistency analysis](../reports/2026-10-12-pose-front-09-consistency-recovery.md)\n23\tidentifies nested mutable aliasing and generation/coordination boundaries.\n24\t\n25\tCached graph copies include shallow Nodes.Attributes, misses are not coordinated\n26\tper key, and the single last graph can churn across projects. These are code-founded\n27\tobservations, not measured corruption by a current consumer or a native race.\n28\tA client-owned MCP stdio stream cannot be attached by an unrelated CLI. Existing\n29\tsessions/cursors/registry mutexes do not prove worktree IPC or write serialization.\n30\t\n31\t## Decision\n32\t\n33\tPropose these boundaries for review, without choosing wire/schema details yet:\n34\t\n35\t- Extract one typed domain interface for admitted operations. CLI/MCP adapt syntax\n36\t  and rendering, with common results/coverage/generation/error semantics. Preserve\n37\t  the adapter-not-fork principle; remove the POSE subprocess only where extraction\n38\t  is qualified. Legitimate scoped Git and external Harness execution remain separate.\n39\t- Preserve project selection and actual authority/assurance. Read-only planning or\n40\t  typed CLI handoff does not authorize writes; sharing code, process UID or stdio\n41\t  does not create Execution Identity, signature or human presence. Active-context\n42\t  discovery keeps authorized logical IDs and never reveals unauthorized roots.\n43\t- Keep validation orchestration outside arbitrary tools/call execution. Native\n44\t  structural/read-only evaluations and explicitly admitted operations use the\n45\t  domain; project check execution still requires its existing request/approval/\n46\t  Harness contract. Scheduling, agents, workflow retries and notifications stay\n47\t  outside the POSE core. Bounded admission/in-flight work here is evaluation of\n48\t  requested calls, not a durable workflow queue or an autonomous scheduler.\n49\t- Expose effectively immutable graph views or deep defensive copies at mutable\n50\t  compatibility boundaries. Keys identify worktree, content, parser/engine/schema\n51\t  and complete dependencies/generation; current authorization/verdict is not\n52\t  inferred from a cache hit. Coordinate one production per key, budget retained\n53\t  bytes/pins/waiters per worktree and globally and measure allocations/RAM.\n54\t- Preserve the scoped Git batch-reader decision. Residence does not authorize\n55\t  a shared cat-file stream, persistent/global reader pool or shell expansion.\n56\t  Any future change of its ownership/lifetime needs a separate structural decision.\n57\t- Make runtime optional, identified by user + canonical worktree/Git identity +\n58\t  engine/build/protocol/schema/capabilities. project_id/common Git directory alone\n59\t  cannot distinguish mutable worktrees. A separate protected local IPC endpoint\n60\t  may host this domain; platform transport (Unix socket/named pipe or equivalent),\n61\t  authentication/permissions and compatibility require resolution before code.\n62\t  Never take a client's MCP stdio connection or claim a static doctor sees it.\n63\t- Support explicit attach/start/status/stop/restart and standalone/CI fallback.\n64\t  Restart/update must not inherit stale generation/authority. An ambiguous committed\n65\t  write requires receipt/reconcile before retry through another process; query\n66\t  fallback validates inputs. Watch events are hints; B14/B15/B34 own publication,\n67\t  coordination and coherent read generations, including external edits.\n68\t- Bound requested evaluation concurrency by category/worktree and globally, with\n69\t  CPU quota/affinity, memory/IO/child-process and admission metrics. Protect short\n70\t  queries during heavy work and preserve deterministic output. Fix budgets and\n71\t  benchmark thresholds on comparable corpora before adoption, rather than changing\n72\t  defaults from one historical sample or claiming p95 from three observations.\n73\t\n74\t## Consequences\n75\t\n76\tB31 yields standalone semantic reuse, B33 supplies isolated/bounded graph views,\n77\tand B35 consumes both after B07/B12/B14/B15/B32/B34 prerequisites. API/caches can\n78\tbe qualified without requiring a resident service; B35 stays opt-in after delivery.\n79\tThe exact IPC/protocol, compatible GateResult/envelopes, platform support, cache\n80\taccounting/view ownership and lifecycle/QoS thresholds remain acceptance work.\n81\tNo new root enumeration, command, default, workflow queue or policy is adopted.\n82\tHistorical benchmark numbers and the duration of documentary checks do not prove\n83\truntime speedup. Consumers retain existing mutable contracts until qualified\n84\tmigration, and the source/effects/assurance remain authoritative.\n85\t\n86\t## Alternatives rejected\n87\t\n88\t- Mirror all .pose into RAM at startup: loads cold history and retains repeated\n89\t  parsing/copying/algorithms without establishing freshness or a memory budget.\n90\t- Reuse the MCP client's stdio as shared IPC: the stream belongs to its initiating\n91\t  client and supplies neither attach compatibility nor authority for another CLI.\n92\t- Fork CLI rules in MCP/runtime: improves transport at the cost of divergent\n93\t  result, validation and authorization semantics.\n94\t- Treat watcher events or mtime/clean as proof: misses external changes and cannot\n95\t  bind a gate/write to complete current inputs.\n96\t- Run arbitrary validation checks in a new in-process MCP helper: bypasses the\n97\t  established execution-identity/approval/Harness boundary.\n98\t- Mandatory autostart and unlimited work/cache: removes CI/standalone fallback,\n99\t  risks memory growth and query starvation without a measured benefit.\n100\t\n101\t## Review triggers\n102\t\n103\tBefore B31/B33/B35 start, settle interface/compatibility, caller mapping, local IPC\n104\tplatform/identity, budgets and benchmark thresholds. Revisit if a scoped reader\n105\tlifetime changes or a new external execution capability is proposed. Track the\n106\texpiring operational review in knowledge:adr-shared-worktree-runtime-review.\n107\t\n108\t## Amendment: bounded, isolated graph caches (B33)\n109\t\n110\tSpec `pose-bounded-isolated-graph-caches` fixes the cache accounting and view\n111\townership this ADR left to acceptance work. Rebuildable derived data (the parsed\n112\tdelivery graph, the design-delta report, the test catalog) goes through one cache\n113\tin the domain package; the paths a commit changed are read by the governed capture,\n114\tbounded and not cached:\n115\t\n116\t- **Bytes, not entries.** Each value reports its retained size; the cache evicts\n117\t  the least recently used entries under a global budget and a budget per owner (a\n118\t  worktree root). The parsed-graph budget is 192 MiB global and 96 MiB per\n119\t  worktree. The size is estimated by walking every field of the value (strings,\n120\t  slices, maps, pointers), so no variable-sized field is charged a constant; the\n121\t  estimate is calibrated against the measured heap of the real index (it errs\n122\t  high, about 2.3 times). A value larger than its budget, and a value its producer\n123\t  declares unretainable, is served and neither retained nor copied for retention.\n124\t  An entry a reader pins (a view in progress) stays charged after eviction until the\n125\t  reader lets go, and the production reservation is held until the result is\n126\t  published, so what the cache keeps alive is what it accounts for.\n127\t- **Work in flight.** A production reserves bytes before it runs, globally and per\n128\t  owner, and waits (or leaves with its context) until they fit; the number of\n129\t  concurrent productions, of queued keys (globally and per owner) and of waiters per\n130\t  key is capped, a waiter that leaves gives its place back, and a caller refused for\n131\t  lack of capacity gets `ErrCacheBusy` on every consumer: overload is an error the\n132\t  caller sees, never a production outside the cache. Copies run outside the lock, one of a\n133\t  few copy slots each, so a large copy never delays another key. The clone a caller\n134\t  keeps after it returns is the caller's memory and is outside the budget; a reader\n135\t  that only looks up fields uses a view and keeps nothing.\n136\t  The same holds for the input a caller read before asking and the clone being made\n137\t  for it: they are the caller's memory, and bounding them is a recorded follow-up. A\n138\t  shared result stays charged until its last waiter has copied it. A gate that needs\n139\t  the test catalog treats an overloaded cache as incomplete validation, not as an\n140\t  empty catalog.\n141\t  Every retained entry is also charged its metadata (the entry, its list node, the\n142\t  lookup slots, 256 bytes), its key and its owner, so many small entries cannot exceed the\n143\t  budget while the payload counters stay within it. Publication (the waiters a result has, the removal of the flight, the release of the\n144\t  reservation) is one critical section. The one read whose output is unbounded by\n145\t  nature, the paths of a commit, is bounded as above.\n146\t- **Key.** Parsed graphs are keyed by engine version, schema and the digest of the\n147\t  bytes: identical bytes cannot yield a different graph, so two worktrees holding\n148\t  the same index share one entry and none is served another's graph; the same size\n149\t  or modification time proves nothing. The test catalog is keyed by root and the\n150\t  digest of the validation matrix and expires after 30 seconds, because it reads\n151\t  the working tree; a long-lived server can serve a catalog built before a test\n152\t  changed for up to those 30 seconds when the matrix did not change (the accepted\n153\t  freshness window), never longer. The paths a commit changed are read by the governed capture, which audits every read and so is not served from a cache: the read is bounded instead, in output (4 MiB) and in paths (100,000), and a larger commit answers unknown and marks the governed read unknown.\n154\t  The design-delta report is keyed by root and the digest of its subject and\n155\t  options, metered per worktree, assessed once per key, and retained only when\n156\t  complete: a partial or unknown report comes from a bound or a failed read that a\n157\t  later call must recompute.\n158\t- **Misses.** A miss is produced once per key however many callers ask together;\n159\t  the production runs outside the lock, so one key never delays another. A waiter\n160\t  whose context ends leaves without disturbing the producer or the other waiters.\n161\t  A failure is never remembered (no negative caching): the next call produces\n162\t  again. Producers and waiters are bounded by the callers themselves; no pool or\n163\t  queue is created.\n164\t- **Isolation.** Every value handed out is a deep copy: the retained one is never\n165\t  shared. The design-delta report is copied by reflection, so a field added later is\n166\t  isolated without anyone remembering it; the graph keeps a hand-written copy\n167\t  because it is hot, and a reflection test holds it to the same standard. Read-only consumers that only look up a few fields use a view\n168\t  (`ViewDeliveryIntegrity`), which hands out the retained graph without a copy and\n169\t  forbids modifying or keeping it; the cache never writes a retained value. The\n170\t  aliasing the audit could not reproduce is reproduced and fixed: the attribute\n171\t  maps of the graph's nodes and the refs of the roadmap criteria were shared with\n172\t  the cache.\n173\t- **Not caches of authority.** Nothing here remembers a verdict, a satisfaction or\n174\t  an authority; gates and writes read current inputs. The Git batch reader of the\n175\t  earlier ADR stays scoped: no global pool or shared stream is introduced.\n176\t\n177\tCost, measured on the real 10.9 MB index: a deep copy per hit costs about 7 ms\n178\tand 18 MB of allocation where the shallow copy cost 1 ms and 5.7 MB; the view costs\n179\tabout 39 KB. That is the price of isolation, paid only by consumers that need a\n180\tmutable graph.\n181\t\n182\t## Amendment: common operations and the typed handoff (B31)\n183\t\n184\tSpec `pose-common-domain-and-cli-mcp-handoff` fixes how an MCP route shares code\n185\twith the CLI, without a resident service:\n186\t\n187\t- **Five route classes.** Every MCP tool is declared one of: domain-direct,\n188\t  cli-wrapper, read-only-planning, admitted-write, external-handoff. A test keeps\n189\t  the declaration equal to the advertised catalog, so a tool cannot ship without a\n190\t  class, and checks that the tools classed admitted-write are exactly the four\n191\t  governance write points (`pose_action_open`, `pose_action_resolve`,\n192\t  `pose_action_fulfil`, `pose_review_attest`), which revalidate authority, assurance,\n193\t  policy and subjects themselves. Other tools that start an agent or record a run\n194\t  (`pose_delegate_*`) or request a gate (`pose_validate_*`) carry their own catalog\n195\t  risk classes and are not admitted writes of the governance state. The MCP-Conductor-Harness boundary (`pose_validate_*`,\n196\t  `conductor_run_*`) is external-handoff: `tools/call` never becomes the execution\n197\t  of a project's checks.\n198\t- **No second binary for read-only operations the CLI package owns.** The domain\n199\t  wrappers run a registered command function in process when it is read-only and\n200\t  bounded (`suggest`, `followups`), and only when that answers as the spawn of the\n201\t  installed binary would: not when `POSE_EXECUTABLE` names another binary, and not\n202\t  when the file the server started from has been replaced since (`pose update` writes\n203\t  the new engine at the same path), because a spawned run then uses the updated engine\n204\t  and the server still holds the old one in memory (spec\n205\t  `pose-mcp-server-survives-self-update` R2). In those cases the wrapper spawns as it\n206\t  always did. The function is the single source: results,\n207\t  errors and coverage are the CLI's. The contract of the spawned run is kept: a\n208\t  non-zero exit is an error carrying the command's message, and a deadline or a\n209\t  cancellation is an execution failure with no verdict (the run cannot be killed, so\n210\t  it finishes in the background and writes nothing; a caller that has already\n211\t  cancelled never starts it, a completion that races a cancellation is discarded, and\n212\t  the runs alive at once, abandoned ones included, are capped so that repeated\n213\t  timeouts cannot accumulate work). The gates keep the process\n214\t  boundary: they are long, can be killed on a deadline, and must not leave a half\n215\t  verdict. Spawn attempts and in-process runs are counted so the extraction is\n216\t  evidenced by count, not by promise.\n217\t- **A typed handoff, not an execution.** A closeout plan with steps left carries an\n218\t  `OperationHandoff`: the operation and scope, the CLI invocation as structured\n219\t  arguments (no shell line), the plan, spec, revision, policy and generation digests\n220\t  it was computed from, the authority it needs, the arguments the performer still owes and the handoff does\n221\t  not carry (the `--reviewer` execution identity when a mechanical attestation is\n222\t  pending), how the outcome comes back and when it stops being valid, and\n223\t  `executes: false`. The request id is derived from the\n224\t  operation, scope and plan digest, so a stale handoff is not mistaken for the\n225\t  current one. It names the project the caller selected, by its logical id and the\n226\t  digest of its root and never by a path (the MCP contract exposes no filesystem\n227\t  root), and does not retarget itself. It carries no secret, grants no authority and infers no context:\n228\t  `pose close --apply` takes no context flag, its binding is the full plan digest it\n229\t  names (the digest the apply compares, with its `sha256:` prefix), and the performer\n230\t  verifies the authority and revision with a fresh `pose context` in the target root\n231\t  before applying. A plan that is terminal, has any blocked step or blocker, or whose\n232\t  read view is unknown has none.\n233\t- **Not shared by sharing code.** stdio is not an IPC channel shared between\n234\t  clients; an MCP transport gains no Execution Identity, signature or human presence\n235\t  of the CLI by calling the same function.\n236\t\n237\t## Related specs\n238\t\n239\t- [B31](../specs/2026-10-14-pose-common-domain-and-cli-mcp-handoff.md)\n240\t- [B33](../specs/2026-10-14-pose-bounded-isolated-graph-caches.md)\n241\t- [B35](../specs/2026-10-14-pose-optional-worktree-runtime.md)\n242\t\n243\t## Amendment: transport, identity and slicing of the worktree runtime (B35)\n244\t\n245\tDecided 2026-11-09 with the maintainer, before the functional start of B35:\n246\t\n247\t- **Transport.** A stream socket in the user's own directory: a Unix domain socket\n248\t  (Linux, macOS), mode 0600 in a 0700 directory owned by the user, whose peer is\n249\t  checked by the operating system's credentials (`SO_PEERCRED`, `LOCAL_PEERCRED`)\n250\t  before any byte is read. No TCP, no token, no network surface. Windows has no\n251\t  named-pipe implementation in this repository (the standard library has none and\n252\t  the repository takes no dependency for it): the runtime reports itself\n253\t  unsupported there and every command runs standalone. That is a declared\n254\t  limitation, not a silent one, and a named pipe with the owner's ACL is the\n255\t  follow-up that lifts it.\n256\t- **Identity.** user (uid) + canonical worktree (symlinks resolved) + Git common\n257\t  directory + engine version/build + wire protocol + schema. The endpoint name is\n258\t  a digest of the location part only (user, worktree, Git common directory), so two\n259\t  worktrees or two users never share an endpoint; the engine part is compared at the\n260\t  handshake, field by field, and a difference is refused naming it, so that a new CLI\n261\t  that finds an old process after an update is refused by name instead of being\n262\t  given a different endpoint and never learning the old one is there.\n263\t- **Authority.** The runtime of slice 1 hosts no domain operation: it answers\n264\t  handshake, status and stop. It confers no authority, signature or presence, and a\n265\t  client that attaches gains nothing it did not have standalone.\n266\t- **Slicing.** B35 is delivered in three specs, each reviewed on its own:\n267\t  `pose-optional-worktree-runtime` (identity, endpoint, handshake, lifecycle,\n268\t  standalone fallback: R1-R4), `pose-worktree-runtime-resident-cache` (R5, R7: the\n269\t  resident reuse of the B33 views, reconciliation, typed results) and\n270\t  `pose-worktree-runtime-qos-and-measurement` (R6, R8: budgets, short queries\n271\t  protected from heavy checks, measured thresholds). Budgets and thresholds are\n272\t  fixed from a measured synthetic corpus with a declared environment, never from a\n273\t  historical sample.\n274\t\n275\t## Amendment: what the optional worktree runtime delivered, and where it departs (B35, 2026-11-10)\n276\t\n277\tThe three slices are done and each was reviewed independently: `pose-optional-worktree-runtime`\n278\t(identity, protected endpoint, handshake, lifecycle, standalone fallback),\n279\t`pose-worktree-runtime-resident-cache` (hosted read checks, typed results, resident memo) and\n280\t`pose-worktree-runtime-qos-and-measurement` (admission lanes and the measurement), with two engine\n281\tspecs that the measurements called for (`pose-index-input-binding-reads-each-transfer-once`,\n282\t`pose-governance-audit-skips-pure-git-reads`). The statement above that \"the runtime of slice 1\n283\thosts no domain operation\" was true of slice 1 only. What stands now, and what differs from the\n284\tdecisions of this ADR:\n285\t\n286\t- **Hosted operations.** The runtime hosts five read checks (`review-check`, `closeout-check`,\n287\t  `artifact-check`, `surface-check`, `roadmap-check`) through `pose runtime run`, as the user that\n288\t  started it, in its worktree; a hosted request carries the caller's identity and is refused,\n289\t  before any handler runs, when it does not match the runtime's engine, build, protocol, schema\n290\t  or worktree; the caller's environment digest must equal the runtime's. They are not writes, but\n291\t  they may refresh derived files as they do standalone (published atomically); a command that may\n292\t  have run (deadline, lost reply) is never repeated elsewhere unasked, which is how the decision\n293\t  \"an ambiguous committed write requires a receipt before a retry\" is met for these commands.\n294\t  Hosted commands go through the domain's own entry (`mainCommand`), not a new typed interface:\n295\t  the typed-domain extraction of the first bullet of the decision stays with B31.\n296\t- **Caches.** The B33 views are not what the runtime reuses (the measurements showed they do not\n297\t  address the cost). It keeps, between commands, the output of the Git reads that are pure\n298\t  functions of object ids or of the commit HEAD named when they ran, keyed by the commit, the\n299\t  content of the shallow file and of the repository config, with a byte budget and\n300\t  least-recently-used eviction; no watcher exists, so \"watch events are hints\" is met by not\n301\t  using them: freshness comes from the key, and nothing about mtime or a clean tree is taken as\n302\t  proof. The scoped Git batch reader is untouched.\n303\t- **Budgets and QoS.** Admission is by two lanes (`short`, `heavy`) with slots and bounded queues\n304\t  and a bound on the memo; the global concurrency bound is the sum of the slots. **Not delivered:**\n305\t  CPU quota or affinity awareness, and per-command memory or I/O limits; the runtime bounds what\n306\t  it admits and keeps, not what each command consumes. The measurement records latency, the\n307\t  runtime's resident set and CPU time per scenario, including two simultaneous heavy checks, on one\n308\t  environment; it is not a general benchmark.\n309\t- **Platforms.** Unix domain socket on Linux and macOS (credentials of the peer from the\n310\t  operating system); Windows runs every command standalone and `pose runtime status` says so.\n311\t  The named pipe is a follow-up.\n312\t- **Status.** The decision stands as amended for the delivered scope; what is not delivered above\n313\t  is not adopted and needs its own spec before it is claimed.\n314\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"[private-session]","uuid":"9f11f443-5fa8-44de-9974-4a421b372b22","timestamp":"2026-11-10T21:35:13.763Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/var/tmp/pose-work-go/pose-review-run-1183365784/worktree/.pose/adr/2026-10-14-shared-domain-and-optional-worktree-runtime.md","content":"# ADR: Shared domain and optional worktree runtime\n\n## Status\n\nAccepted for the delivered scope — 2026-11-10 (proposed 2026-10-14, for B31/B33/B35). The\noptional worktree runtime (B35) was delivered in three slices, each reviewed independently, and\nthe last amendment below states what stands and where it departs from the decisions above:\na protected Unix-socket endpoint with identity and handshake, five hosted read checks with typed\nresults, a resident memo of pure Git reads, and admission lanes with a measurement. Not adopted,\nand needing a spec of their own before they are claimed: CPU quota and affinity, per-command\nmemory and I/O limits, a named pipe for Windows, routing the checks to the runtime by default.\nPreserve accepted ADRs for active-context discovery, scoped Git readers, obligations and the\nMCP-Conductor-Harness validation boundary.\n\n## Context\n\nThe [performance analysis](../reports/2026-10-12-pose-front-08-resident-performance.md)\nseparates computation, transport and freshness costs. CLI-backed Store adapters\nspawn the binary with a fixed deadline; MCP persistence alone does not remove\nrepeated algorithms or graph copies. The [UX analysis](../reports/2026-10-12-pose-front-07-operational-ux.md)\nshows plan/context composition and planning handoff gaps while P15 read parity\nalready works. The [consistency analysis](../reports/2026-10-12-pose-front-09-consistency-recovery.md)\nidentifies nested mutable aliasing and generation/coordination boundaries.\n\nCached graph copies include shallow Nodes.Attributes, misses are not coordinated\nper key, and the single last graph can churn across projects. These are code-founded\nobservations, not measured corruption by a current consumer or a native race.\nA client-owned MCP stdio stream cannot be attached by an unrelated CLI. Existing\nsessions/cursors/registry mutexes do not prove worktree IPC or write serialization.\n\n## Decision\n\nPropose these boundaries for review, without choosing wire/schema details yet:\n\n- Extract one typed domain interface for admitted operations. CLI/MCP adapt syntax\n  and rendering, with common results/coverage/generation/error semantics. Preserve\n  the adapter-not-fork principle; remove the POSE subprocess only where extraction\n  is qualified. Legitimate scoped Git and external Harness execution remain separate.\n- Preserve project selection and actual authority/assurance. Read-only planning or\n  typed CLI handoff does not authorize writes; sharing code, process UID or stdio\n  does not create Execution Identity, signature or human presence. Active-context\n  discovery keeps authorized logical IDs and never reveals unauthorized roots.\n- Keep validation orchestration outside arbitrary tools/call execution. Native\n  structural/read-only evaluations and explicitly admitted operations use the\n  domain; project check execution still requires its existing request/approval/\n  Harness contract. Scheduling, agents, workflow retries and notifications stay\n  outside the POSE core. Bounded admission/in-flight work here is evaluation of\n  requested calls, not a durable workflow queue or an autonomous scheduler.\n- Expose effectively immutable graph views or deep defensive copies at mutable\n  compatibility boundaries. Keys identify worktree, content, parser/engine/schema\n  and complete dependencies/generation; current authorization/verdict is not\n  inferred from a cache hit. Coordinate one production per key, budget retained\n  bytes/pins/waiters per worktree and globally and measure allocations/RAM.\n- Preserve the scoped Git batch-reader decision. Residence does not authorize\n  a shared cat-file stream, persistent/global reader pool or shell expansion.\n  Any future change of its ownership/lifetime needs a separate structural decision.\n- Make runtime optional, identified by user + canonical worktree/Git identity +\n  engine/build/protocol/schema/capabilities. project_id/common Git directory alone\n  cannot distinguish mutable worktrees. A separate protected local IPC endpoint\n  may host this domain; platform transport (Unix socket/named pipe or equivalent),\n  authentication/permissions and compatibility require resolution before code.\n  Never take a client's MCP stdio connection or claim a static doctor sees it.\n- Support explicit attach/start/status/stop/restart and standalone/CI fallback.\n  Restart/update must not inherit stale generation/authority. An ambiguous committed\n  write requires receipt/reconcile before retry through another process; query\n  fallback validates inputs. Watch events are hints; B14/B15/B34 own publication,\n  coordination and coherent read generations, including external edits.\n- Bound requested evaluation concurrency by category/worktree and globally, with\n  CPU quota/affinity, memory/IO/child-process and admission metrics. Protect short\n  queries during heavy work and preserve deterministic output. Fix budgets and\n  benchmark thresholds on comparable corpora before adoption, rather than changing\n  defaults from one historical sample or claiming p95 from three observations.\n\n## Consequences\n\nB31 yields standalone semantic reuse, B33 supplies isolated/bounded graph views,\nand B35 consumes both after B07/B12/B14/B15/B32/B34 prerequisites. API/caches can\nbe qualified without requiring a resident service; B35 stays opt-in after delivery.\nThe exact IPC/protocol, compatible GateResult/envelopes, platform support, cache\naccounting/view ownership and lifecycle/QoS thresholds remain acceptance work.\nNo new root enumeration, command, default, workflow queue or policy is adopted.\nHistorical benchmark numbers and the duration of documentary checks do not prove\nruntime speedup. Consumers retain existing mutable contracts until qualified\nmigration, and the source/effects/assurance remain authoritative.\n\n## Alternatives rejected\n\n- Mirror all .pose into RAM at startup: loads cold history and retains repeated\n  parsing/copying/algorithms without establishing freshness or a memory budget.\n- Reuse the MCP client's stdio as shared IPC: the stream belongs to its initiating\n  client and supplies neither attach compatibility nor authority for another CLI.\n- Fork CLI rules in MCP/runtime: improves transport at the cost of divergent\n  result, validation and authorization semantics.\n- Treat watcher events or mtime/clean as proof: misses external changes and cannot\n  bind a gate/write to complete current inputs.\n- Run arbitrary validation checks in a new in-process MCP helper: bypasses the\n  established execution-identity/approval/Harness boundary.\n- Mandatory autostart and unlimited work/cache: removes CI/standalone fallback,\n  risks memory growth and query starvation without a measured benefit.\n\n## Review triggers\n\nBefore B31/B33/B35 start, settle interface/compatibility, caller mapping, local IPC\nplatform/identity, budgets and benchmark thresholds. Revisit if a scoped reader\nlifetime changes or a new external execution capability is proposed. Track the\nexpiring operational review in knowledge:adr-shared-worktree-runtime-review.\n\n## Amendment: bounded, isolated graph caches (B33)\n\nSpec `pose-bounded-isolated-graph-caches` fixes the cache accounting and view\nownership this ADR left to acceptance work. Rebuildable derived data (the parsed\ndelivery graph, the design-delta report, the test catalog) goes through one cache\nin the domain package; the paths a commit changed are read by the governed capture,\nbounded and not cached:\n\n- **Bytes, not entries.** Each value reports its retained size; the cache evicts\n  the least recently used entries under a global budget and a budget per owner (a\n  worktree root). The parsed-graph budget is 192 MiB global and 96 MiB per\n  worktree. The size is estimated by walking every field of the value (strings,\n  slices, maps, pointers), so no variable-sized field is charged a constant; the\n  estimate is calibrated against the measured heap of the real index (it errs\n  high, about 2.3 times). A value larger than its budget, and a value its producer\n  declares unretainable, is served and neither retained nor copied for retention.\n  An entry a reader pins (a view in progress) stays charged after eviction until the\n  reader lets go, and the production reservation is held until the result is\n  published, so what the cache keeps alive is what it accounts for.\n- **Work in flight.** A production reserves bytes before it runs, globally and per\n  owner, and waits (or leaves with its context) until they fit; the number of\n  concurrent productions, of queued keys (globally and per owner) and of waiters per\n  key is capped, a waiter that leaves gives its place back, and a caller refused for\n  lack of capacity gets `ErrCacheBusy` on every consumer: overload is an error the\n  caller sees, never a production outside the cache. Copies run outside the lock, one of a\n  few copy slots each, so a large copy never delays another key. The clone a caller\n  keeps after it returns is the caller's memory and is outside the budget; a reader\n  that only looks up fields uses a view and keeps nothing.\n  The same holds for the input a caller read before asking and the clone being made\n  for it: they are the caller's memory, and bounding them is a recorded follow-up. A\n  shared result stays charged until its last waiter has copied it. A gate that needs\n  the test catalog treats an overloaded cache as incomplete validation, not as an\n  empty catalog.\n  Every retained entry is also charged its metadata (the entry, its list node, the\n  lookup slots, 256 bytes), its key and its owner, so many small entries cannot exceed the\n  budget while the payload counters stay within it. Publication (the waiters a result has, the removal of the flight, the release of the\n  reservation) is one critical section. The one read whose output is unbounded by\n  nature, the paths of a commit, is bounded as above.\n- **Key.** Parsed graphs are keyed by engine version, schema and the digest of the\n  bytes: identical bytes cannot yield a different graph, so two worktrees holding\n  the same index share one entry and none is served another's graph; the same size\n  or modification time proves nothing. The test catalog is keyed by root and the\n  digest of the validation matrix and expires after 30 seconds, because it reads\n  the working tree; a long-lived server can serve a catalog built before a test\n  changed for up to those 30 seconds when the matrix did not change (the accepted\n  freshness window), never longer. The paths a commit changed are read by the governed capture, which audits every read and so is not served from a cache: the read is bounded instead, in output (4 MiB) and in paths (100,000), and a larger commit answers unknown and marks the governed read unknown.\n  The design-delta report is keyed by root and the digest of its subject and\n  options, metered per worktree, assessed once per key, and retained only when\n  complete: a partial or unknown report comes from a bound or a failed read that a\n  later call must recompute.\n- **Misses.** A miss is produced once per key however many callers ask together;\n  the production runs outside the lock, so one key never delays another. A waiter\n  whose context ends leaves without disturbing the producer or the other waiters.\n  A failure is never remembered (no negative caching): the next call produces\n  again. Producers and waiters are bounded by the callers themselves; no pool or\n  queue is created.\n- **Isolation.** Every value handed out is a deep copy: the retained one is never\n  shared. The design-delta report is copied by reflection, so a field added later is\n  isolated without anyone remembering it; the graph keeps a hand-written copy\n  because it is hot, and a reflection test holds it to the same standard. Read-only consumers that only look up a few fields use a view\n  (`ViewDeliveryIntegrity`), which hands out the retained graph without a copy and\n  forbids modifying or keeping it; the cache never writes a retained value. The\n  aliasing the audit could not reproduce is reproduced and fixed: the attribute\n  maps of the graph's nodes and the refs of the roadmap criteria were shared with\n  the cache.\n- **Not caches of authority.** Nothing here remembers a verdict, a satisfaction or\n  an authority; gates and writes read current inputs. The Git batch reader of the\n  earlier ADR stays scoped: no global pool or shared stream is introduced.\n\nCost, measured on the real 10.9 MB index: a deep copy per hit costs about 7 ms\nand 18 MB of allocation where the shallow copy cost 1 ms and 5.7 MB; the view costs\nabout 39 KB. That is the price of isolation, paid only by consumers that need a\nmutable graph.\n\n## Amendment: common operations and the typed handoff (B31)\n\nSpec `pose-common-domain-and-cli-mcp-handoff` fixes how an MCP route shares code\nwith the CLI, without a resident service:\n\n- **Five route classes.** Every MCP tool is declared one of: domain-direct,\n  cli-wrapper, read-only-planning, admitted-write, external-handoff. A test keeps\n  the declaration equal to the advertised catalog, so a tool cannot ship without a\n  class, and checks that the tools classed admitted-write are exactly the four\n  governance write points (`pose_action_open`, `pose_action_resolve`,\n  `pose_action_fulfil`, `pose_review_attest`), which revalidate authority, assurance,\n  policy and subjects themselves. Other tools that start an agent or record a run\n  (`pose_delegate_*`) or request a gate (`pose_validate_*`) carry their own catalog\n  risk classes and are not admitted writes of the governance state. The MCP-Conductor-Harness boundary (`pose_validate_*`,\n  `conductor_run_*`) is external-handoff: `tools/call` never becomes the execution\n  of a project's checks.\n- **No second binary for read-only operations the CLI package owns.** The domain\n  wrappers run a registered command function in process when it is read-only and\n  bounded (`suggest`, `followups`), and only when that answers as the spawn of the\n  installed binary would: not when `POSE_EXECUTABLE` names another binary, and not\n  when the file the server started from has been replaced since (`pose update` writes\n  the new engine at the same path), because a spawned run then uses the updated engine\n  and the server still holds the old one in memory (spec\n  `pose-mcp-server-survives-self-update` R2). In those cases the wrapper spawns as it\n  always did. The function is the single source: results,\n  errors and coverage are the CLI's. The contract of the spawned run is kept: a\n  non-zero exit is an error carrying the command's message, and a deadline or a\n  cancellation is an execution failure with no verdict (the run cannot be killed, so\n  it finishes in the background and writes nothing; a caller that has already\n  cancelled never starts it, a completion that races a cancellation is discarded, and\n  the runs alive at once, abandoned ones included, are capped so that repeated\n  timeouts cannot accumulate work). The gates keep the process\n  boundary: they are long, can be killed on a deadline, and must not leave a half\n  verdict. Spawn attempts and in-process runs are counted so the extraction is\n  evidenced by count, not by promise.\n- **A typed handoff, not an execution.** A closeout plan with steps left carries an\n  `OperationHandoff`: the operation and scope, the CLI invocation as structured\n  arguments (no shell line), the plan, spec, revision, policy and generation digests\n  it was computed from, the authority it needs, the arguments the performer still owes and the handoff does\n  not carry (the `--reviewer` execution identity when a mechanical attestation is\n  pending), how the outcome comes back and when it stops being valid, and\n  `executes: false`. The request id is derived from the\n  operation, scope and plan digest, so a stale handoff is not mistaken for the\n  current one. It names the project the caller selected, by its logical id and the\n  digest of its root and never by a path (the MCP contract exposes no filesystem\n  root), and does not retarget itself. It carries no secret, grants no authority and infers no context:\n  `pose close --apply` takes no context flag, its binding is the full plan digest it\n  names (the digest the apply compares, with its `sha256:` prefix), and the performer\n  verifies the authority and revision with a fresh `pose context` in the target root\n  before applying. A plan that is terminal, has any blocked step or blocker, or whose\n  read view is unknown has none.\n- **Not shared by sharing code.** stdio is not an IPC channel shared between\n  clients; an MCP transport gains no Execution Identity, signature or human presence\n  of the CLI by calling the same function.\n\n## Related specs\n\n- [B31](../specs/2026-10-14-pose-common-domain-and-cli-mcp-handoff.md)\n- [B33](../specs/2026-10-14-pose-bounded-isolated-graph-caches.md)\n- [B35](../specs/2026-10-14-pose-optional-worktree-runtime.md)\n\n## Amendment: transport, identity and slicing of the worktree runtime (B35)\n\nDecided 2026-11-09 with the maintainer, before the functional start of B35:\n\n- **Transport.** A stream socket in the user's own directory: a Unix domain socket\n  (Linux, macOS), mode 0600 in a 0700 directory owned by the user, whose peer is\n  checked by the operating system's credentials (`SO_PEERCRED`, `LOCAL_PEERCRED`)\n  before any byte is read. No TCP, no token, no network surface. Windows has no\n  named-pipe implementation in this repository (the standard library has none and\n  the repository takes no dependency for it): the runtime reports itself\n  unsupported there and every command runs standalone. That is a declared\n  limitation, not a silent one, and a named pipe with the owner's ACL is the\n  follow-up that lifts it.\n- **Identity.** user (uid) + canonical worktree (symlinks resolved) + Git common\n  directory + engine version/build + wire protocol + schema. The endpoint name is\n  a digest of the location part only (user, worktree, Git common directory), so two\n  worktrees or two users never share an endpoint; the engine part is compared at the\n  handshake, field by field, and a difference is refused naming it, so that a new CLI\n  that finds an old process after an update is refused by name instead of being\n  given a different endpoint and never learning the old one is there.\n- **Authority.** The runtime of slice 1 hosts no domain operation: it answers\n  handshake, status and stop. It confers no authority, signature or presence, and a\n  client that attaches gains nothing it did not have standalone.\n- **Slicing.** B35 is delivered in three specs, each reviewed on its own:\n  `pose-optional-worktree-runtime` (identity, endpoint, handshake, lifecycle,\n  standalone fallback: R1-R4), `pose-worktree-runtime-resident-cache` (R5, R7: the\n  resident reuse of the B33 views, reconciliation, typed results) and\n  `pose-worktree-runtime-qos-and-measurement` (R6, R8: budgets, short queries\n  protected from heavy checks, measured thresholds). Budgets and thresholds are\n  fixed from a measured synthetic corpus with a declared environment, never from a\n  historical sample.\n\n## Amendment: what the optional worktree runtime delivered, and where it departs (B35, 2026-11-10)\n\nThe three slices are done and each was reviewed independently: `pose-optional-worktree-runtime`\n(identity, protected endpoint, handshake, lifecycle, standalone fallback),\n`pose-worktree-runtime-resident-cache` (hosted read checks, typed results, resident memo) and\n`pose-worktree-runtime-qos-and-measurement` (admission lanes and the measurement), with two engine\nspecs that the measurements called for (`pose-index-input-binding-reads-each-transfer-once`,\n`pose-governance-audit-skips-pure-git-reads`). The statement above that \"the runtime of slice 1\nhosts no domain operation\" was true of slice 1 only. What stands now, and what differs from the\ndecisions of this ADR:\n\n- **Hosted operations.** The runtime hosts five read checks (`review-check`, `closeout-check`,\n  `artifact-check`, `surface-check`, `roadmap-check`) through `pose runtime run`, as the user that\n  started it, in its worktree; a hosted request carries the caller's identity and is refused,\n  before any handler runs, when it does not match the runtime's engine, build, protocol, schema\n  or worktree; the caller's environment digest must equal the runtime's. They are not writes, but\n  they may refresh derived files as they do standalone (published atomically); a command that may\n  have run (deadline, lost reply) is never repeated elsewhere unasked, which is how the decision\n  \"an ambiguous committed write requires a receipt before a retry\" is met for these commands.\n  Hosted commands go through the domain's own entry (`mainCommand`), not a new typed interface:\n  the typed-domain extraction of the first bullet of the decision stays with B31.\n- **Caches.** The B33 views are not what the runtime reuses (the measurements showed they do not\n  address the cost). It keeps, between commands, the output of the Git reads that are pure\n  functions of object ids or of the commit HEAD named when they ran, keyed by the commit, the\n  content of the shallow file and of the repository config, with a byte budget and\n  least-recently-used eviction; no watcher exists, so \"watch events are hints\" is met by not\n  using them: freshness comes from the key, and nothing about mtime or a clean tree is taken as\n  proof. The scoped Git batch reader is untouched.\n- **Budgets and QoS.** Admission is by two lanes (`short`, `heavy`) with slots and bounded queues\n  and a bound on the memo; the global concurrency bound is the sum of the slots. **Not delivered:**\n  CPU quota or affinity awareness, and per-command memory or I/O limits; the runtime bounds what\n  it admits and keeps, not what each command consumes. The measurement records latency, the\n  runtime's resident set and CPU time per scenario, including two simultaneous heavy checks, on one\n  environment; it is not a general benchmark.\n- **Platforms.** Unix domain socket on Linux and macOS (credentials of the peer from the\n  operating system); Windows runs every command standalone and `pose runtime status` says so.\n  The named pipe is a follow-up.\n- **Status.** The decision stands as amended for the delivered scope; what is not delivered above\n  is not adopted and needs its own spec before it is claimed.\n","numLines":314,"startLine":1,"totalLines":314}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_014b2xYpw25BCDMfwatLf2U1","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
